# SPSC Queue
 
A high-performance single-producer single-consumer queue implemented in C++.
 
---
 
## Features
 
- Lock-free design using C++ atomics
- Cache-line aligned head/tail indices to prevent false sharing
- Local caching of indices to minimize atomic loads
- Tuned memory ordering — `acquire/release` on index updates, `relaxed` on buffer accesses
---
 
## Performance
 
Tested on macOS (Apple Silicon), release build (`-O2`), single-threaded.
 
| Test | Wall Time | CPU Time |
|---|---|---|
| 500 `emplace_back`, queue size 5000 | 1,013 ns | 1,016 ns |
 
**~2 ns per push** — essentially L1 cache speed (1-4 ns), which is the target for an SPSC queue. Wall time and CPU time are nearly identical, indicating no OS scheduling interference.
 
---
## Optimizations
 
**False sharing prevention**
Head and tail indices are padded to separate cache lines with `alignas(64)`. Without this, a producer write to tail invalidates the cache line containing head, causing unnecessary cache coherence traffic between cores.
 
**Memory ordering**
Index updates use `acquire/release` ordering. Buffer accesses use `relaxed` — synchronization happens through the index, so the buffer itself needs no fence. This avoids expensive `mfence` instructions from `seq_cst`.
 
**Local index caching**
Each thread caches the other's index locally and only reloads atomically when the queue appears full or empty. The cached value is a plain stack variable — zero atomic overhead on the fast path.
 
---
 
## Environment
 
- Platform: macOS (Apple Silicon)
- Compiler: Clang, `-O2`
- Queue size: 5000 elements
- Benchmark library: Google Benchmark
 